ion. | About $410 billion for FY2027: the $372 billion reference implies roughly $186 billion in second-half revenue if split approximately evenly; a 20% uplift to that half adds about $37 billion, implying roughly $409 billion. This is an approximate forecast, with Q2 acceleration supportive but not separately quantified.\\\\\\\\n2026-07-27 2012 2013 About $405 billion. Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion and Q2 guidance of $91 billion imply roughly 11.5% sequential growth; carrying that forward gives about $101.5 billion in Q3 and $113 billion in Q4, or roughly $387 billion for the year, with booked supply commitments and demand evidence supporting a modest uplift above $400 billion. | About $409 billion, or roughly $410 billion: starting from the $372 billion reference, a 20% uplift applied to the second half adds approximately $37 billion. This is above the rung 2 threshold of $400 billion and below rung 1's $450 billion threshold.\\\\\\\\n2026-07-28 2163 2171 About $401 billion: Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion plus Q2 guidance of $91 billion, carrying the embedded 11.5% sequential growth through Q3 and Q4, implies about $387 billion. I add a modest uplift for demand, deployment and investment signals, discounted because multiyear targets are not FY2027 revenue in hand and supply and China-related constraints remain. | Roughly $415 billion for FY2027. The $372 billion carried-growth reference is raised by about $37 billion for SemiAnalysis’s forecast that H2 FY2027 Data Center revenue will exceed analyst estimates by 20%, with the Q2 guide and acceleration commentary supporting continued strength.\\\\\\\\n2026-07-29 2290 2293 About $400 billion: Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion and Q2 guidance of $91 billion, carried forward at the implied 11.5% sequential growth, produce a roughly $387 billion run-rate; demand and booked-supply signals support a modest uplift toward $400 billion, but are not quantified as incremental FY2027 revenue. | About $415 billion for FY2027. The roughly 