nts as of July 26 plus $18 billion committed for the rest of FY2027), which is rung 3. The implied amount is higher: the\\\\\\\\n09-09 7522 ed company reference is about $43 billion ($25 billion of commitments as of July 26 plus $18 billion earmarked for the rest of FY2027), which sits in rung 3. The forecast is higher: the up-to-$10\\\\\\\\n09-10 7754 pany-based reference is about $43 billion: $25 billion of reported commitments plus $18 billion earmarked for the rest of FY2027. That is rung 3. I forecast roughly $60 billion outstanding or com\\\\\\\\n09-11 7907 pany-based reference is about $43 billion: $25 billion of reported commitments as of July 26, 2026, plus $18 billion committed for the remainder of FY2027. That sits in rung 3. I forecast roughly\\\\\\\\n09-12 8048 pany-based reference is about $43 billion: $25 billion of equity investment commitments reported as of July 26, 2026, plus the $18 billion earmarked for the remainder of FY2027. That reference is\\\\\\\\n09-13 8139 he company-based reference is $43 billion: $25 billion of equity investment commitments reported as of July 26, 2026, plus $18 billion earmarked for the rest of FY2027. That falls in rung 3 ($30 \\\\\\\\n09-14 8316 he company-based reference is $43 billion: $25 billion of equity investment commitments reported as of July 26 plus $18 billion earmarked for the rest of FY2027. That reference sits in rung 3. Th\\\\\\\\n09-15 8452 he company-based reference is $43 billion: $25 billion of equity investment commitments reported as of July 26 plus $18 billion committed for the rest of FY2027. That reference sits in rung 3. Th\\\\\\\\n09-16 8630 About $43 billion, with some possibility of modest additional commitments: the disclosed $25 billion plus the $18 billion committed for the remainder of FY2027. I do not count the mo\\\\\\\\n09-17 8746 About $43 billion, derived from the $25 billion reported commitment balance plus the $18 billion committed for the remainder of FY2027. The reported and estimated amounts already inv\\\\\\\\n09-18 8965 About $43 billion, derived from the $25 billion reported commitment balance plus the $18 billion committed for the rest of FY2027. The evidence does not establish a quantified additi\\\\\\\\n09-19 9074 The evidence implies roughly $43 billion or less as the most defensible commitment envelope at the FY2027 filing: the $25 billion reported balance plus the $18 billion remainder commitment. That \\\\\\\\n09-20 9175 $40–45 billion, centered near $43 billion: carry forward the reported $25 billion commitment balance together with the stated $18 billion remainder-of-FY2027 commitment, without adding later deal\\\\\\\\nTIGHT 25\\\\\\\\nDRAW 3 v41\\\\\\\\n5978 2026-08-30 More than $40 billion of FY2028 CPU revenue, derived by more than doubling the stated $20 billion FY2027 expectation. That supports rung 2, but the evidence does not establish a level of at least $50 billion.\\\\\\\\n6213 2026-08-31 More than $40 billion of CPU revenue in FY2028, derived by more than doubling the $20 billion FY2027 expectation. No Vera-only amount is supplied, and the evidence does not quantify the outcome at $50 billion or more.\\\\\\\\n6435 2026-09-01 The guidance implies FY2028 CPU revenue above $40 billion, but does not state a precise figure or quantify Vera’s share separately. With no new evidence to move the Vera-specific forecast across the $50 billion boundary, the implied outcome remains rung 2.\\\\\\\\n6661 2026-09-03 The best forecast remains rung 2 ($40 billion to less than $50 billion), using the FY2028 CPU-revenue outlook as the available proxy; the evidence does not quantify Vera's share or support a more precise Vera-only figure.\\\\\\\\n6856 2026-09-04 The outlook implies total FY2028 CPU revenue above $40 billion ($20 billion in FY2027, more than doubled), but gives no figure for Vera’s share. No Vera-specific amount can be derived, so the existing forecast remains rung 2.\\\\\\\\n6964 2026-09-05 The evidence implies FY2028 total CPU revenue above $40 billion, but gives no Vera-specific revenue figure or share. Accordingly, the carried forecast remains rung 2, using the broad-CPU outlook as a proxy rather than treating it as a Vera-specific result.\\\\\\\\n7153 2026-09-06 No Vera-specific figure is supplied. Using the total CPU outlook of more than $40 billion as the proxy, as before, implies rung 2; the evidence does not establish Vera's share of that total.\\\\\\\\n7284 2026-09-07 The stated expectation implies more than $40 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2028. Applying that aggregate as a proxy leaves Vera near the $40 billion rung-2 boundary, but the evidence provides no Vera-specific allocation.\\\\\\\\n7382 2026-09-08 The supplied expectation implies total FY2028 CPU revenue above $40 billion. Because the evidence does not state what share is Vera, it does not establish a Vera-specific figure; the existing roughly $40 billion proxy remains the forecast basis.\\\\\\\\n7502 2026-09-09 The stated FY2027 total CPU expectation is $20 billion, and NVIDIA expects FY2028 total CPU revenue to more than double it, implying over $40 billion for CPUs overall. No Vera share is stated, so the evidence supports only the prior roughly $40 billion total-CPU proxy for Vera, not a separately derived Vera revenue figure.\\\\\\\\n7740 2026-09-10 The $20 billion FY2027 total-CPU anchor doubled gives a roughly $40 billion gross proxy, while the direct Vera statement specifies doubling but not Vera's baseline. Discounting for Vera's unestablished share of total CPU revenue and unproven shipment volume implies a central FY2028 Vera estimate in the mid-$30 billions.\\\\\\\\n7893 2026-09-11 The evidence implies a mid-$30-billion Vera FY2028 outcome, carried from the prior estimate: the broader CPU outlook points above $40 billion, while Vera is said to double from an unquantified FY2027 base and its share of total CPU revenue is not stated. That estimate remains below the $40 billion rung-2 threshold.\\\\\\\\n8037 2026-09-12 Roughly the high-$30 billions for FY2028: discount the stated more-than-$40-billion target because the evidence does not show Vera shipment volume in hand, while giving weight to the stated broad deployment footprint and the expanding server-CPU market.\\\\\\\\n8134 2026-09-13 High-$30 billions (roughly $38–39 billion): the more-than-$40-billion management target is discounted because the evidence does not show Vera shipment volume in hand. That estimate remains below the $40 billion rung-2 boundary.\\\\\\\\n8297 2026-09-14 Discount the greater-than-$40-billion management target because the evidence does not show Vera shipment volume in hand. The implied outcome remains in the high-$30 billions, below $40 billion and within rung 3.\\\\\\\\n8433 2026-09-15 Approximately the high-$30 billions (around $38 billion): the more-than-$40-billion management target is discounted because the evidence does not show Vera shipment volume in hand. The implied figure remains below the $40 billion rung boundary.\\\\\\\\n8616 2026-09-16 The management forecast implies more than $40 billion from a $20 billion FY2027 base. Discounting that target because the evidence does not show Vera shipment volume in hand, while crediting the broad deployment expectations, implies roughly $38–40 billion for FY2028.\\\\\\\\n8753 2026-09-17 About $38–40 billion: discount the more-than-$40-billion management target for the still-unshipped product and lack of quantified volume in hand. This remains below the $40 billion rung-2 boundary.\\\\\\\\n8953 2026-09-18 The management target implies more than $40 billion, but discounted for execution and the absence of Vera shipment volume in hand, the implied outcome remains around $38–40 billion. That is below the $40 billion threshold for rung 2 and within rung 3.\\\\\\\\n9071 2026-09-19 Roughly $38–39 billion for FY2028: the management reference implies more than $40 billion, but I discount it for the lack of Vera volume in hand and the new competitive-performance evidence. Broad deployment expectations and CPU-market growth provide some offset, without establishing NVIDIA’s share.\\\\\\\\n9166 2026-09-20 Roughly $38–39 billion for FY2028: the stated more-than-double expectation points above $40 billion, but is discounted for a forward Vera target without shipment volume in hand, with the competitive evidence adding execution risk.\\\\\\\\nTIGHT 0\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"exit_code\\\\\\\":0,\\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"completed\\\\\\\"}}\\\\ndocs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/codex-r45.stream:93:{\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"item.started\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"item\\\\\\\":{\\\\\\\"id\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"item_48\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"command_execution\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"command\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"python3 -B -c 'import sqlite3,json,pathlib,re,collections,decimal\\\\\\\\nD=decimal.Decimal\\\\\\\\ndef db(i,name='\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\''views'\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\''):\\\\\\\\n c=sqlite3.connect((pathlib.Path(f'\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\''poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/run/old-{i}/prod'\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\'')/(name+'\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\''.sqlite'\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\'')).resolve().as_uri()+'\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\''?mode=ro'\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\'',uri=True)\\\\\\\\n c.row_factory=sqlite3.Row\\\\\\\\n return c\\\\\\\\ndef req(a,j):\\\\\\\\n c=a.execute('\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\''select * from model_calls where call_id=?'\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\'',(j['\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\''call_id'\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\''],)).fetchone()\\\\\\\\n assert c and c['\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\''request_sha256'\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\'']==j['\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\''request_sha256'\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\''] and c['\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\''purpose'\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\'']=='\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\''j