{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_33","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n 'Escap|escaped as|fetch mutant|Measured on|port nothing|Budget|stored question|opening its|source shows|format 2|noscript' docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/RELEASE-PAGE.md; nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/RELEASE-BUILD.md | sed -n '119,135p'\"","aggregated_output":"12:  - the package gains that page (format 2);\n38:    `<`, `>`, `&`, U+2028 and U+2029 escaped as `<`, `>`, `&`, ` ` and ` `. JSON reads them back unchanged.\n66:    - each source shows title, publisher and day. It is a link only when its URI starts with `https://` or `http://` (with `rel`\n80:| The browser blocks inline script | nothing renders. The page shows a `<noscript>` line saying it needs script. It holds no forecast to leak |\n103:1. **Determinism.** `render_page` of the fixture data twice gives the same bytes. The page holds the data bytes, escaped as specified.\n104:2. **Escaping.** A fixture quote holding `</script><img src=x onerror=alert(1)>` and a U+2028:\n128:  the slice-1 command under format 2.\n135:- its article shows exactly the stored question, \"Open\", \"Forecast unavailable\" and \"1284 pieces of evidence\";\n136:- opening its details shows `e2295`'s stored summary;\n144:**The fetch mutant.** The same check on a copy of the `old-1` page with a fetch inserted must fail. That proves the check sees a\n146:- **Measured on this Mac (Chrome 158, headless, scratchpad probe):** a fetch blocked by the CSP produces **no**\n151:- **The mutant fetch targets `http://127.0.0.1:9/library`,** a port nothing listens on, never Alex's service on 8788.\n155:**Budget,** set before the first control: the `old-1` page renders its question list within 3 s of load in headless Chrome, and its\n   119\t  - `PACKAGE_FILES = {'manifest.json', 'release-data.json'}`.\n   120\t- **`ALLOWED`:** the closed key set per record type.\n   121\t\n   122\t  | Record | Keys |\n   123\t  |---|---|\n   124\t  | envelope | `format`, `allow_list`, `as_of`, `questions` |\n   125\t  | number question | `id`, `question`, `open`, `class` (= `number`), `forecast` (= the marker), `evidence` |\n   126\t  | event or Yes/No question | `id`, `question`, `open`, `class` (`event` or `yes_no`), `answers` (the two stored rung labels, in position order), `answer` (the stored label of the latest judgement's rung on or before the bound, or null), `evidence` |\n   127\t  | evidence in force at the bound | `id`, `one_line`, `quote`, `tier`, `speaker`, `original_who`, `original_day`, `sources` |\n   128\t  | source recorded on or before the bound | `title`, `publisher`, `day`, `uri` |\n   129\t  | manifest | `format`, `allow_list`, `as_of`, `data_sha256` |\n   130\t\n   131\t- **`question_class(views_db, view_id, labels)`** (design §3):\n   132\t  - `event` when the view key's measure is in `identity.EVENT_MEASURES`. The key is read as `select measure from view_keys`, and\n   133\t    only when the table exists;\n   134\t  - `yes_no` when there are exactly two labels which, through `vocabulary.canonical('label', …)`, are `Yes` and `No` in either order;\n   135\t  - `number` otherwise: a view with no key, or one whose bands cannot be resolved.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_34","type":"agent_message","text":"**Verdict: not ready to build.** The package check is sound, but the screen check could pass a page that says “1284 pieces of evidence” and shows only one summary. It could also reject a correct page because five genuine source titles contain “Bullish.” [RELEASE-PAGE.md:134](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/RELEASE-PAGE.md:134) · [RELEASE-CHECK-RESULT.md:145](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/RELEASE-CHECK-RESULT.md:145)\n\n1. **An ordinary source link breaks the zero-request promise.** I counted 1,258 HTTP(S) source entries under `old-1/v8`. Clicking one can navigate from the local file to the publisher. `noopener noreferrer` suppresses opener and referrer information. It still permits navigation. Opening `<details>` itself needs no request. For the promise written in §0, render the source address as plain text and check that expanding the evidence creates no navigable links. The `file://` test can then run with Chrome’s normal file restrictions. [RELEASE-PAGE.md:16](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/RELEASE-PAGE.md:16) · [RELEASE-PAGE.md:64](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/RELEASE-PAGE.md:64) · [CSP navigation directives](https://www.w3.org/TR/CSP3/#directives-navigation)\n\n2. **The specified JSON escaping leaves the closing tag intact.** I checked the file bytes. Line 38 gives the same literal characters on both sides of “escaped as.” A source containing `</script>` can therefore end the data block before the page script reads it. Specify the literal JSON escape sequences `\\u003c`, `\\u003e`, `\\u0026`, `\\u2028` and `\\u2029`. Declare UTF-8 at the start of the file and put the CSP before the style, script and embedded data. Test the existing fixture in the browser with its evidence expanded. Its exact quote must be visible as text, with no image element created. The proposed “no second `<script>` start” check misses the closing tag, and parsing JSON alone never exercises the proposed `innerHTML` mutant. [RELEASE-PAGE.md:37](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/RELEASE-PAGE.md:37) · [RELEASE-PAGE.md:104](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/RELEASE-PAGE.md:104) · [HTML script parsing](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/scripting.html#restrictions-for-contents-of-script-elements) · [CSP meta placement](https://www.w3.org/TR/CSP3/#meta-element)\n\n3. **Keep byte-for-byte rendering and format 2. Update the checks that depend on format 1.** Read all three files once through the open directory handle. Hash and compare those same page bytes against `render_page(raw_data)` using the same data bytes that passed the store check. That certifies the inspected copy. Adding a required file and manifest member justifies `/2` while the data allow-list stays `/1`. The existing acceptance script pins `/1`, and two helpers that deliberately alter data refresh only the data hash. Their format-2 copies must also regenerate the page and its hash so the forged forecast still reaches the store comparison. Add the converse case too: a correct page with only `page_sha256` wrong must fail for that exact reason. Every proposed page-tampering test refreshes that hash, so deleting the hash check currently escapes them. [release.py:454](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/src/hivemind/release.py:454) · [release_check.py:24](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/release_check.py:24) · [release_check.py:128](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/release_check.py:128) · [test_release.py:180](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/tests/test_release.py:180) · [RELEASE-PAGE.md:109](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/RELEASE-PAGE.md:109)\n\n4. **Check what the reader sees both before and after expanding `v8`.** Pin the exact question, “How much Data Center segment revenue will NVIDIA report for FY2027?”, alongside the three stated labels. For the expanded card, freeze the expected ordered evidence and source values from the accepted package before the run. Compare the displayed entries with that expectation, including the full Morningstar quote and attribution. This catches missing, duplicated or misplaced entries and permits “Bullish” exactly where the source used it. Close and reopen the card once to catch a second copy being appended. Apply the same discipline to the two dated cards: pin the day and question, require “Answer: Yes,” expand their evidence, and check the known explanation phrases there too. A collapsed page-text search cannot inspect content the script has yet to create. [FORECAST-UNAVAILABLE-DESIGN.md:134](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/FORECAST-UNAVAILABLE-DESIGN.md:134) · [RELEASE-PAGE.md:69](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/RELEASE-PAGE.md:69) · [release_check.py:173](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/release_check.py:173) · [RELEASE-CHECK-RESULT.md:60](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/RELEASE-CHECK-RESULT.md:60)\n\n5. **Credit the fetch test only when Chrome identifies the attempted connection.** The Chrome 158 probe recorded in the design answers the CDP question for this Mac: the blocked fetch produced two log errors and zero `Network.requestWillBeSent` events. Recompute the altered inline script’s CSP hash so the test script actually runs. Require the normal card to render and the security event to name the intended destination and `connect-src`. A stale script hash would otherwise earn credit for blocking the whole script. Port 9 also has an independent browser block, so a generic fetch error there proves too little. Enable observation before navigation and keep it active through expansion. If “zero console errors” remains a condition, include `Runtime.consoleAPICalled` for `console.error`. [RELEASE-PAGE.md:130](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/RELEASE-PAGE.md:130) · [RELEASE-PAGE.md:144](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/RELEASE-PAGE.md:144) · [Fetch port blocking](https://fetch.spec.whatwg.org/#port-blocking) · [CDP console events](https://chromedevtools.github.io/devtools-protocol/tot/Runtime/#event-consoleAPICalled)\n\n6. **Correct two browser acceptance conditions.** A blocked inline script leaves browser scripting enabled, so `<noscript>` stays hidden. If the reader should see a fallback in that case, put ordinary visible text in the page and replace it after successful rendering. The 3-second and size budgets **are** set before the first screen control. Start the timer at navigation and stop when the full expected list is visible. Starting at `load` can omit the work of parsing and rendering the embedded data. The state table also promises a phone-width and dark-scheme check that §6 never performs. Either define its viewport and observable pass condition or remove the acceptance claim. [RELEASE-PAGE.md:79](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/RELEASE-PAGE.md:79) · [RELEASE-PAGE.md:155](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/RELEASE-PAGE.md:155) · [HTML `noscript` behavior](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/scripting.html#the-noscript-element)\n\nThe separate release page fits D758 and requires no POC change. The text filter is optional for D756. I would leave it out of this slice so acceptance covers the reader’s actual task: opening a question and seeing all its sources with the forecast withheld. This was a read-only review. The Chrome observation above comes from the probe recorded in the design. [D756:11833](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11833) · [D758:11881](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11881) · [RELEASE-PAGE.md:59](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/RELEASE-PAGE.md:59)"}}
